  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1200 PM CST Fri Nov 27 2026\n\nMidday infrared satellite imagery depicted widespread cirrus\nacross western Kansas. Despite this limitation of insolation and\nthe weak sun angle, northwest downslope trajectories through the\nlower troposphere will allow temperatures to warm into the lower\n60s this afternoon. \n\nStrong shortwave is consistently forecast by models to reach\nthe Pacific NW 6 pm Friday, race across the Rockies Saturday,\nand arrive in Kansas Saturday evening. Associated cold front\nwill be quite strong, and is progged to be racing through SW KS\n4-8 pm Saturday. Along with neighbors, increased wind grids to\nthe 90% ile of NBM, expanded the inherited high wind watch to\ninclude the favored US 83 corridor, increased gusts to near\n60 mph, and added areas of blowing dust to the weather grids.\nLatest HRRR runs and other CAMs suggest wind gusts of 55-60 mph\nshould be commonplace for several hours Saturday afternoon into\nevening. Tonight's night shift will need to upgrade to a high \nwind warning, and also consider expanding the end time to \nperhaps 06z/midnight- and expanding the warning eastward.\nOnset of nocturnal cooling will decouple higher momentum aloft \nto some extent, but gusts in excess of 50 mph are expected to \ncontinue well into the evening hours. \n\nScattered showers, mostly rain, some wet snow, are expected\nSaturday evening. Trough appears to have enough amplitude and\nstrength to generate scattered rain showers as the primary lift\nquickly passes Saturday evening. Shower coverage will favor\nareas north and east of Dodge City, where some CAM guidance\nsuggests up to 0.10 inch of rain is possible locally. Thickness\nand 850 mb temperatures will crash Saturday night, cold enough\nto support snow by 6 am Sunday, but most/all precipitation is\nexpected to be ended by then. \n\nThere is no real cold air supply for this system to tap, so\ntemperatures will only be briefly cooler, reduced to normal, in\nthe upper 40s and lower 50s Sunday afternoon. Another healthy\nwarming trend is expected heading into December, from the 50s\nMonday, and well into the 60s Wednesday. High confidence SW KS\nwill remain dry Sunday through Wednesday. NBM pops increase into\nthe chance category with the arrival of the next wave Thursday,\nbut clearly any precipitation would be rain with no cold air\navailable. \n\nPer the 6-10 day temperature outlooks, high confidence\ntemperatures will remain well above normal well into December,\nwith no cold/arctic air in sight over the next 10 days.",
